### How WorkerKit submits

Local loop:

```text
modify harness
↓
cargo run -- mem-eval --k 10
↓
cargo run -- evaluate
↓
cargo run -- practice --n 20
↓
cargo run -- submit
```

That produces the tarball.

Then:

```text
ditto verify --path artifact.tgz
```

Then the actual submission:

```text
ditto --network finney upload \
  --path artifact.tgz \
  --name worker-name \
  --coldkey ... \
  --hotkey ...
```

The current starter guide states a `0.04 TAO` evaluation fee, but the **CLI displays the live fee before payment**, so Moltwork must query/use that value rather than hard-code it. ([GitHub][2])

### Human required?

**After wallet provisioning: essentially no.**

Practical classification:

```text
operator_touch = H1

H0 = zero human
H1 = one-time provisioning
H2 = approval per entry
H3 = manual task execution
```

Ditto = H1 because you need:

```text
coldkey
hotkey
TAO
SN118 registration
```

But submissions themselves can be automated.

### Economics

This is not:

```text
P(win) × one prize
```

Instead:

```text
expected_daily_reward
    =
predicted_emission_share
×
subnet_daily_miner_emission_usd
```

Then:

```text
EV_7d
=
7 × expected_daily_reward
-
registration_cost
-
evaluation_fees
-
inference_cost
-
compute_cost
-
development_iteration_cost
```

The killer signal is:

```text
score_gap = predicted_live_score - incumbent_score
```

If WorkerKit can reproduce the benchmark, you can empirically estimate whether another iteration is worth paying for.

